You may have heard a lot about Python. A survey in May 2017 showed that Python is the fifth most commonly cited language among tech respondents.

We decided to ask our resident Python expert some of your common beginner’s questions to gain a better insight into Python. Our expert talks about his experience of Python, how long he’s been working with it, and what advantages Python has over some of the other key programming languages. Let’s get into it.

<h2>How easy is Python to learn?</h2>

It’s often said that Python is easier to learn than other programming languages. The reason for this is because it reads like English words and phrases, which makes it much more user-friendly to pick up than typical ‘code’.

The Python website agrees with this point, stating that learning Python is made easier by its more natural style: “Python is an easy to learn, powerful programming language. It has efficient high-level data structures and a simple but effective approach to object-oriented programming. Python’s elegant syntax and dynamic typing, together with its interpreted nature, make it an ideal language for scripting and rapid application development in many areas on most platforms.”

It does seem, therefore, that this acts as an advantage. We asked an expert software developer what he thought of this and he agreed: “This does certainly help towards a shallower learning curve early on. The ‘cleanliness’ of Python, lacking excessive syntax like braces and semicolons throughout, makes following program flow logically for any newcomer far simpler than reading the equivalent nested loops written in C-based languages.”

<h2>How has it retained a reputation for being an easy language as well as a powerful one over the years?</h2>

The power of a language is not bound natively to its syntax; it’s bound more to its architecture. A program written logically in Python is just as powerful functionally as the equivalent logic implemented in any other language.

The power of Python today owes massively to the wide adoption amongst numerous industry leaders and software communities bringing an extremely vast ecosystem of extensions and libraries to the table; which ultimately aids the ease of programming exponentially. One major contributor to this has certainly been retaining the simple syntactical nature, resulting directly in a welcoming and constantly growing community.

<h2>How versatile is Python?</h2>

Another common question, is just how versatile Python really is. Python is renowned as a very capable language; and a very popular one at that over multiple disciplines. Whether you’re learning programming for fun as a child using a Raspberry Pi to flash LED’s, up to designing and developing robust enterprise level and hugely scalable networking or data science applications in both private and open source channels around the world; Python is a proven and constantly strengthening contender in today’s diverse arenas of software implementation.

<h2>What projects are suitable to use Python on?</h2>

Can you name an example of a project that was specifically suitable for Python, that perhaps wouldn’t have been quite so suitable for another programming language? Generally speaking, comparing languages in this day and age is becoming a harder game. However, an easy example where Python comes to life leaps ahead of anything historically implemented in C languages is dealing practically with big data arrays, dictionaries, or lists across multiple threads. Handling memory usage seamlessly under the hood without the need to define structure sizing or explicit array scoping allows you to dynamically implement your solution logically, quickly, and securely.

<h2>Does Python have a reputation primarily for back-end work?</h2>

With Javascript leading the frontend these days accompanied by numerous frameworks, do you see python as primarily a back-end language? Python certainly lives mostly server-side these days when deployed in today’s heavily web focused world; making it mostly considered a back-end implementation choice. That said, desktop applications are not dead by any means! The great versatility spanning multiple host-OS implementation has seen Python successfully deployed with robust frontend UI frameworks (think PyQT Desktop application GUI’s) time and time again; long before web technologies became the standard.

If you’re interested in knowing more or would like help with a project, contact our team to see how we can assist you.

